{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-68213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-68213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-68213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-68213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-68213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{9}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-68213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-339797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-339797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-339797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-339797" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-339797" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x})=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-73539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-68215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-68215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\frac{x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-68215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-68215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-68215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-68215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-302820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-302820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{2x}{x}+\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot [{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2+3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (2+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-302820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-302820" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-302820" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-105433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-105433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )=4{cos}^{2}⁡0=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =1\\cdot 4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-105433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-105433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-105433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\text{DNE}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-105433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-340124" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340124" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}({\\theta }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-340672" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340672" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-73546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-73546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡0}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4} & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "037a9a81d66e735858297d54854b5d611f47719f226839c9f30fd92ade896351"} {"question" "q-340150" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "q-340150" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/state.json" "file_sha256" "3b9f53c33a6398d370719c468543bf506f6165edc5e243e8980e7225899c505f"} {"question" "e-94" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/example-94.json" "file_sha256" "7b73def64447d60c8e141507ffe97b26c110ee2222dfb50216b320f1ba4d73ec"} {"question" "e-94" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that as $x \\to 0,$ both the numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/example-94.json" "file_sha256" "7b73def64447d60c8e141507ffe97b26c110ee2222dfb50216b320f1ba4d73ec"} {"question" "e-6167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta \\cot \\theta}{4 \\cos^2 \\theta}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/example-6167.json" "file_sha256" "fbba6d737e2af37f0430f5887865eaeac057d38ae3fd7ed19c0bf34d69eab87c"} {"question" "e-6167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{1}{4 \\cos \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\cos 0} = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042622/example-6167.json" "file_sha256" "fbba6d737e2af37f0430f5887865eaeac057d38ae3fd7ed19c0bf34d69eab87c"}] "usage" {"e-6167" [] "e-94" []} "basis" 1574 "retractions" [[17592186078299 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x}$."] [17592186078299 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x \\to 0$, both the numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{2\\sin x}{3x} &= lim_(x → 0)(\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot lim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\sin x}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186078250 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta \\cot \\theta}{4 \\cos^2 \\theta}$."] [17592186078250 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{θ\\cot θ}{4\\cos^{2} θ} &= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{θ}{4\\cos^{2} θ} \\cdot \\cot θ) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{θ}{4\\cos^{2} θ} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{θ}{\\sin θ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\cos θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{θ}{\\sin θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{4\\cos θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0}$,\nwhich is an indeterminate form. Rewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n$lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{θ}{\\sin θ}|= (1)/((lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin θ}{θ})); = \\frac{1}{1}; = 1$\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{1}{4 \\cos \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\cos 0} = \\dfrac14$.\nTherefore, we have\n$lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{θ}{\\sin θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{4\\cos θ}|= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}$."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
